<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The daemon's bundle. It exists for exactly one reason: the microphone TCC grant attaches to a
  signed bundle identity, and a bare executable would have the grant attributed to whatever terminal
  launched it (D11). Everything here is either what makes that grant possible or what keeps the
  bundle from becoming an application in any other sense (§12, §13): no main nib, no document
  types, no URL schemes.

  The icon is the one thing here that serves neither purpose, and it is not a contradiction of §13.
  What §13 rules out is a DOCK icon — a tile that can be clicked, that takes focus from the terminal
  dicta is about to type into. LSUIElement below still forbids that. This key is the artwork macOS
  shows where the bundle is named whether it wants to be an application or not: System Settings →
  Privacy & Security → Microphone, which is the one screen a user of dicta actually has to visit,
  and where the alternative is a blank sheet of paper beside a request for the microphone.

  CFBundleIdentifier is the anchor. It is the same string as `Paths.bundleID`, which names the
  Application Support directory the socket and the record live in — one identity, one directory, one
  grant. A test asserts the two have not drifted.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Dicta</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Dicta</string>
    <!-- The SwiftPM product is named `Dicta`; bundle.sh copies it in under this name. -->
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>Dicta</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>dev.personal.dicta</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <!-- `AppIcon.icns`, copied in by bundle.sh from Resources/. Both bundles carry the SAME icon:
         the daemon and the menu are one tool in two processes, and the Privacy list and Finder are
         not the place to explain that. -->
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.1.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <!-- No dock icon, no menu bar, no windows (D11, §12). Without this the daemon would appear in
         the dock and in ⌘-tab, and a dictation would be one stray click away from stealing focus
         from the terminal it is about to type into. -->
    <key>LSUIElement</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>14.0</string>
    <!-- Shown in the TCC prompt, once. It names the tool, says what the audio is for, and says where
         it goes — because "Dicta would like to access the microphone" with no sentence under it is
         exactly the prompt people deny. English, like everything else in this repository. -->
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>dicta records the microphone while you dictate, and types the words into the terminal you pressed the chord in. The audio stays on this Mac and is never uploaded.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
